Show top-level `new.target` results in an FFI warning

  $ . ./setup.sh
  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > let () = [%mel.raw{| new.target |}]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -warn-error +103 ./x.ml
  File "./x.ml", line 1, characters 24-25:
  1 | let () = [%mel.raw{| new.target |}]
                              ^
  Warning 103 [melange-ffi-warning]: FFI warning: Unexpected token `.`
  // Generated by Melange
  'use strict';
  
  
  (new.target);
  /*  Not a pure module */

Currently this also warns when the raw expression is emitted inside a
generated function, even though the resulting JavaScript is valid.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > let f () = [%mel.raw{| new.target |}]
  > let () = Js.log (f ())
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -warn-error +103 ./x.ml | tee x.js
  File "./x.ml", line 1, characters 26-27:
  1 | let f () = [%mel.raw{| new.target |}]
                                ^
  Warning 103 [melange-ffi-warning]: FFI warning: Unexpected token `.`
  // Generated by Melange
  'use strict';
  
  
  function f(param) {
    return new.target;
  }
  
  console.log(f());
  
  module.exports = {
    f,
  }
  /*  Not a pure module */
  $ node x.js
  undefined

No warning if used within its correct scope

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > let () = [%mel.raw{|
  >   function Foo() {
  >     if (!new.target) {
  >       throw new TypeError("calling Foo constructor without new is invalid");
  >     }
  >   } |}]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx ./x.ml
  // Generated by Melange
  /* This output is empty. Its source's type definitions, externals and/or unused code got optimized away. */
